Commits that cannot be in a glass backdrop do not resample it.

BackgroundDamageObserver marks a cached backdrop dirty whenever any other
surface commits inside the glass's padded box. GTK and Qt clients commit with
full damage, and Qt commits every mapped window of a process while any
animation runs, so that test alone had glass sampled and blurred again many
times a second with nothing behind it changing. Left out now:

- For window glass, a layer on the top or overlay level: it is drawn after
  every window. A bar redrawing a few times a second cost about 130
  megapixels a second for one terminal on a 5120x2160 output.
- For window glass, another window that lies beside it and not behind it
  (further than NEIGHBOUR_REACH_PX from its box).
- For layer glass, whatever is drawn after the layer: windows for the
  background and bottom levels, layers on higher levels, and layers later in
  the monitor's list on the same level. Text on a layer above its own glass
  panes had the panes resampled on every frame of an animation.
- The padding that reaches onto a neighbouring monitor: a window or layer
  that is wholly on its monitor samples that monitor only, so a full-screen
  commit on the next output no longer counts as overlapping it.
- The same padding when the glass damages its sample region: a full-screen
  glass layer redrawn on one monitor damaged a strip of the next one, which
  then drew a frame for each of them (60 a second with nothing changing).

